{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 5994,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fact: in a black hole, all of the mass is\nconcentrated at the singularity at the very center"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6234,
    "dDurationMs": 4706,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fact: every black hole singularity is surrounded\nby an event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10940,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nothing can escape from within the event horizon unless it can travel faster than light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17560,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fact: gravity travels at the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20840,
    "dDurationMs": 6259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does a black hole manage to communicate its gravitational force to the outside universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27099,
    "dDurationMs": 2901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How does gravity escape a black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35169,
    "dDurationMs": 6491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1915 Einstein presented to the world the\nequations behind his general theory of relativity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41660,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which describe gravity not as a traditional\nforce, but rather in terms of the curvature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46270,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the fabric of space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48890,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s theory predicted the existence\nof the ultimate gravitational object: the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53320,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54870,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are objects of such extreme density\nthat the fabric of space is dragged inwards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59120,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at greater than the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61390,
    "dDurationMs": 4839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to Einstein’s theory, any object\nthat reaches such a density has to collapse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66229,
    "dDurationMs": 6271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a point-like singularity of infinite density\nsurrounded by this boundary of no return - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72500,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73880,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "General relativity made many other predictions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76850,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of them is that gravity itself has a speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81070,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein built the equations of GR"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82710,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Equation: G_{\\mu\\nu} + \\Lambda g_{\\mu\\nu}\n= \\kappa T_{\\mu\\nu}"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83710,
    "dDurationMs": 4329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so they would be consistent with his special\ntheory of relativity, which describes how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88039,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lengths and times and other properties depend on how fast you are moving relative to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92719,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93979,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Special relativity enshrined the speed of\nlight as the absolute cosmic speed limit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98249,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tells us that light speed is the maximum\nspeed at which any causal influence can travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104149,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the maximum speed of information - the\nspeed of causality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109119,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Naturally enough, the “c” of the speed\nof light made its way into the equations of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113850,
    "dDurationMs": 1710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115560,
    "dDurationMs": 4129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you use those equations to calculate\nthe speed of various gravitational effects,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119689,
    "dDurationMs": 2941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they also turn out to be the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122630,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example we have gravitational waves - ripples in spacetime caused by certain types of motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128910,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They travel at the speed of light, and that’s\nbeen confirmed when gravitational waves from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133610,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colliding neutron stars reach us at about\nthe same time the corresponding electromagnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139330,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation from the explosion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142190,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this “speed of gravity” also tells\nus how quickly a regular gravitational field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147800,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148800,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine for a moment that the Sun just disappeared."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151220,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you can’t just erase mass, but let’s\npretend that you can for the sake of argument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156860,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would take 8 minutes for us to notice the\nsudden darkness, and the Earth would continue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162080,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to orbit the now-empty patch of space for\nthe same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166270,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would take 8 minutes for the Sun’s deep\nindentation in the fabric of space to smooth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170970,
    "dDurationMs": 4115,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself - in the wake of some pretty crazy\ngravitational waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175085,
    "dDurationMs": 1646,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But here we have a problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176731,
    "dDurationMs": 4719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If gravity travels at the speed of light,\nand all of the mass of a black hole is hidden"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181450,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beneath the event horizon, how does its gravity get out to influence the surrounding universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187200,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shouldn’t a black hole’s event horizon\nprotect the universe from its own malicious influence?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192570,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To answer this we’re going to look at gravity\nin two completely different ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197260,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First we’ll see what Einstein has to say\non the matter, and then we’ll go deeper,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202170,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the speculative realm of quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205610,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starting with good old fashioned general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207940,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no question here - a black hole’s\ngravity doesn’t care about the event horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212750,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214010,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In GR, the gravitational field - the curvature\nof spacetime - has an independent existence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219960,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the mass that causes it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222120,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, when Earth feels the pull of\nthe Sun’s gravity - it’s not directly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226820,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interacting with the Sun itself, it’s interacting\nonly with the local part of the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232230,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233350,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Same for a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234630,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The space around a black hole doesn’t need\nto know about the mass of the central singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238820,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- it only needs to know what the space next\nto it is doing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242440,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s this old analogy of space as a sheet\nof rubber stretched by a heavy mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248820,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not that great an analogy in many ways,\nbut here it’s actually useful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254340,
    "dDurationMs": 5979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rubber at any one point in the sheet doesn’t know about the massive object - it’s being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260319,
    "dDurationMs": 3951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stretched only by the pull of neighboring\npatches of rubber."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264270,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s another way to think about the\naction of gravity: instead of a stretching"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268300,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime, we can think about space as\nflowing towards the massive object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The “speed of space” is just the speed\nof a free-falling, or inertial observer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278080,
    "dDurationMs": 5019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Falling from very far away, an observer and\nthe patch of space that they occupy reach"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283099,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light speed at the event horizon of the black\nhole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286639,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A close analogy to this picture of gravity\nis a river flowing towards a waterfall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291840,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The water is the fabric of space, and it accelerates towards the drop."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296820,
    "dDurationMs": 6767,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At some point it exceeds the swimming speed of any possible fish - that’s the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303587,
    "dDurationMs": 5655,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any fish that passes that point will be carried\nto the singularity - the fall itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309242,
    "dDurationMs": 4128,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s think about what pulls any patch\nof water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313370,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The water at the “event horizon” doesn’t\nknow about the fall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316850,
    "dDurationMs": 4539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s pulled along by the water a little\nahead of it, which in turn is pulled by the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321389,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next adjacent patch and so on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324189,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravity works like the rubber sheet or the\nflowing river."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327259,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One patch of space doesn’t need to see the\nultimate source of the field - it only needs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331629,
    "dDurationMs": 2991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see the next patch along."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334620,
    "dDurationMs": 5868,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Depending on how you think about it, the curvature or the motion of each patch influences the next."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340488,
    "dDurationMs": 3401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This explanation works for the black holes\nof general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343889,
    "dDurationMs": 3229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we know that GR is not the final theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347118,
    "dDurationMs": 4252,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It breaks down at very small distances and\nin very high gravitational fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351370,
    "dDurationMs": 2849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, at the black hole singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354219,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many physicists believe that general relativity\nneeds to be replaced by a theory of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358699,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity to explain the behavior of gravity\nin these circumstances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363290,
    "dDurationMs": 5089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So can gravity escape from a “real” black hole of quantum gravity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368379,
    "dDurationMs": 5021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now in quantum mechanics - or more specifically quantum field theory - forces are mediated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373400,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by particles, not by the geometry of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376930,
    "dDurationMs": 5859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example the electromagnetic force is communicated between charged particles by transferring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382789,
    "dDurationMs": 6151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "virtual photons - ephemeral excitations in\nthe electromagnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388940,
    "dDurationMs": 5029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In theories of quantum gravity, the gravitational force should probably also have a mediating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393969,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle - usually called the graviton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396949,
    "dDurationMs": 1697,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, fine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398646,
    "dDurationMs": 3633,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But doesn’t that make things worse for black\nholes?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402279,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If gravity is really communicated by a particle,\nhow does that particle escape the event horizon?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407759,
    "dDurationMs": 6311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, even in this picture, the event\nhorizon has no way to halt the force of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414070,
    "dDurationMs": 3929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a bit of a misconception in how\nwe think about virtual particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417999,
    "dDurationMs": 4634,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They don’t really travel from one location\nto another, carrying force with them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422633,
    "dDurationMs": 3223,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Virtual particles aren’t localized like\nthat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425856,
    "dDurationMs": 2454,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say two electrons approach each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428310,
    "dDurationMs": 2229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They interact by exchanging a virtual photon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430539,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or more precisely, they exchange the sum of\nall possible virtual photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436129,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those photons don’t follow a well defined path between the interacting particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441020,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They sort of emerge from the electromagnetic\nfield in the broader region occupied by both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446960,
    "dDurationMs": 4799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the electrons, and their summed\neffect leads to a repulsive force between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451759,
    "dDurationMs": 1391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453150,
    "dDurationMs": 6159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if gravity is really mediated by virtual\ngravitons, then those gravitons don’t emerge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459309,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the location of the singularity, and\nthey don’t have to travel through the event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463479,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon to do their work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464909,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The gravitational field around the black hole\nis already abuzz with virtual gravitons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470779,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you might ask why those gravitons themselves don’t get swallowed by the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475979,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s easy - these are virtual particles,\nand in quantum field theory, virtual particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481150,
    "dDurationMs": 2379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are not restricted by the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483529,
    "dDurationMs": 2869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can travel at any speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486398,
    "dDurationMs": 5611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Interactions between particles result from\nthe sum of all virtual particle interactions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492009,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible and Impossible, and the speed of\nlight limit actually emerges in a sort of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498289,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "statistical way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499569,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The overall interaction, along with any information that it communicates has to be sub-light-speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505899,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we’re describing the gravitational\nfield as being built up by virtual gravitons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510729,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the event horizon is no barrier at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514339,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately you still can’t send an SOS message from inside of a black hole that way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519589,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this idea of sending information across\nan event horizon brings us to our last argument,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524899,
    "dDurationMs": 4171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this one works whether we’re talking\nabout the classical gravity of Einstein or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529070,
    "dDurationMs": 2389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some deeper theory of quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531459,
    "dDurationMs": 3081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cosmic speed limit is the speed limit\nof information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534540,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To experience the gravitational effect of\na massive object, the information about the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539310,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "presence of that mass does have to be able to reach you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543220,
    "dDurationMs": 3552,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to be able to “see” that mass,\nat least in principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546772,
    "dDurationMs": 5708,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it might surprise you to learn that you\nactually CAN see the mass of black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The present mass of a black hole is hidden\nbelow the event horizon, but we can see its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558320,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "past mass, and it’s the gravitational effect\nof the past mass that we actually feel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564750,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think about a star collapsing into a black\nhole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567370,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As it approaches the surface that is to become the event horizon, it approaches light speed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572320,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with respect to someone watching from a distance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576170,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to relativity, that means its clock\nslows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579070,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It appears to freeze at the event horizon,\nand the light it emits becomes stretched out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584290,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sapped of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586110,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The star does appear to go black, but really\nthe faintest signals of that collapsing star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592070,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continue to make their way out into the universe over infinite time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596150,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s ignoring the whole Hawking radiation, black hole evaporation thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601160,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we can still “see” the mass of a black\nhole - it’s imprinted on the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606630,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether gravity is communicated by the curvature of spacetime or by virtual gravitons, we maintain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612860,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a causal connection to the mass that generated that gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619070,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And for those of you who love their Penrose\ndiagrams, just think about the source of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623380,
    "dDurationMs": 5939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field as always being in your\npast lightcone - and that has to be outside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629319,
    "dDurationMs": 1161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this last argument also tells us how it\ncan be that a black hole can possess electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636800,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637800,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a black hole swallows electric charge,\nthe electromagnetic field around the black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641930,
    "dDurationMs": 1639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole grows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643569,
    "dDurationMs": 1431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645000,
    "dDurationMs": 4709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because when you look at a charged black hole you still have causal contact with all the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649709,
    "dDurationMs": 1901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge that fell into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651610,
    "dDurationMs": 3669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You interact with the past charge, not the\npresent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655279,
    "dDurationMs": 3751,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the point of view of that charge, it’s\ninside the black hole, but from your point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659030,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of view it’s frozen on the event horizon,\nbut is happily exerting its influence on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664030,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surrounding universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665610,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This whole question is related to another\nvery interesting one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669470,
    "dDurationMs": 3669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a black hole, where is the mass?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673139,
    "dDurationMs": 4252,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A simplistic view says that it’s at the\nsingularity - but that’s not the mass that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677391,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you interact with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678391,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You interact with the local curvature of spacetime, which is produced by the past mass, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683680,
    "dDurationMs": 3339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from your point of view is on the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687019,
    "dDurationMs": 5411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the whole idea of mass is poorly defined in general relativity in part because the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692430,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field itself has energy, and\nso is a source of mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697240,
    "dDurationMs": 5469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get a consistent definition for mass you need to integrate - add up - the contributions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702709,
    "dDurationMs": 3151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to infinite distance from the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705860,
    "dDurationMs": 5599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By that definition the mass of a black hole\nis everywhere - so it’s not surprising that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711459,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can escape the horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713519,
    "dDurationMs": 4461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To sum up - don’t mess around near black\nholes hoping that the event horizon will protect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717980,
    "dDurationMs": 2419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you from the black hole’s gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720399,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many seemingly different pictures all point\nto the same result - the black hole will eat"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724899,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you right up, and even as you’re getting\ncrushed into an infinitesimal point, you can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731389,
    "dDurationMs": 6471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rest assured that your own mass will continue\nto exert its gravitational influence on exterior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737860,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740160,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before getting to comments, we’d\nlike to thank our supports on Patreon. And"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744269,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "today’s special shoutout goes to Alex Kern who’s supporting us at the Quasar Level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749379,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alex, as token of our appreciation, we asked our friends at Center for Computational Astrophysics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755079,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to run a few Universe simulations and track the evolution of the entity known as Alex"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760850,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761970,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In one of those simulations you live 3000\nyears and become emperor of space, and in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766019,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another you star in Interstellar instead of\nMatthew McConaughey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770540,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in all of the simulations you are incredibly generous, interesting, and much loved by all the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776130,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other simulated entities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778160,
    "dDurationMs": 1219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like in this one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779379,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you for your support in this simulation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782519,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our two recent episodes were all about simulating the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786899,
    "dDurationMs": 5861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about the ingenious method for simulating the insane amount of information in the quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792760,
    "dDurationMs": 5689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction with density functional theory,\nand then went from the tiny to the enormous,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798449,
    "dDurationMs": 5111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exploring how we do simulations of everything from the sizes of planets to the size of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803560,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805420,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starting with the quantum, manonthedollar\nasks - given the incredible amount of power"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810459,
    "dDurationMs": 2771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "required to simulate quantum interactions..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813230,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do the ACTUAL interactions also require that\nmuch effort from the universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818420,
    "dDurationMs": 4219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well this is an excellent question - and the answer is that we have no idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822639,
    "dDurationMs": 6021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Badly drawn Turtle has an interesting thought on the matter and I quote: \"That a small part of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828660,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction can be used to \"reconstruct\"\nthe whole wavefunction, or at least the part"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833670,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of it that could be considered to correspond\nwith our reality, is an indicator that there's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838800,
    "dDurationMs": 4889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a much simpler underlying theory on the order\nof complexity of that small part."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843689,
    "dDurationMs": 5441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not that the information of the wavefunction can be compressed below it's \"true\" informational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849130,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "volume, it's that it can be simplified down\nfrom its false, overinflated informational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854380,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "volume.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855380,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Agentdarkboote and Meisam pointed out an error - or at least, a missed detail."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861040,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We said that in density function theory you\nstart with a make-believe system of non-interacting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865900,
    "dDurationMs": 1109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867009,
    "dDurationMs": 6661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Agentdarkboote tells us that you do include\nCoulomb and exchange interactions even in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873670,
    "dDurationMs": 5779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your initial guess, and that the remaining\npart you have to approximate is the electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879449,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "correlation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881019,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks for the details guys - we went deep on this one and may have missed a few points."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887779,
    "dDurationMs": 5711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And speaking of corrections, for our episode\non astrophysical simulations, Mercurius314"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893490,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spotted a serious blunder."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895750,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We said that the room-sized computer used\nto calculate the Apollo trajectories had the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900410,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computing power of a smart phone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902720,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's just not true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904350,
    "dDurationMs": 6770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Apollo mission computers such as the IBM 7090 ran around 100 kiloflops while the GPU of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911120,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a modern smartphone is in the hundreds of gigaflops to teraflop range."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916970,
    "dDurationMs": 2679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we were 5 orders of magnitude out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919649,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a case of me blithely repeating something I heard once without checking the math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924959,
    "dDurationMs": 2721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Please don’t think that this is a habit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927680,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We check a lot of math on this show, but I guess sometimes something sneaks through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932350,
    "dDurationMs": 1830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, not cool Matt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934180,
    "dDurationMs": 2790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t just say dumb stuff that you heard anywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936970,
    "dDurationMs": 2239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s not what we do here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939209,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inspired by the simulations we showed of the\ninevitable collision of the Milky Way with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943389,
    "dDurationMs": 5521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Andomeda Galaxy, Robert Herd asks the following: how can it be that in an expanding universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948910,
    "dDurationMs": 5739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where all the galaxies are rushing away from us, that we could be on such a collision course?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954649,
    "dDurationMs": 3231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well the answer is sort of what you say in\nyour own question: the real universe is more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957880,
    "dDurationMs": 2819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chaotic than implied by simple expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960699,
    "dDurationMs": 3481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we understand that “chaos” very well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964180,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Distant galaxies appear to be moving away\nbecause space on the largest scales is expanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967870,
    "dDurationMs": 1009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evenly everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968879,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But on small scales, local gravitational influences dominate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974189,
    "dDurationMs": 5921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine a bunch of magnets on a rubber sheet - stretch the sheet and the magnets move apart,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980110,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unless they’re close together, then the\nmagnets will obviously form stars and galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985610,
    "dDurationMs": 5159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And speaking of the Milky Way-Andromeda collision, Roli Rivelino points out that way back in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990769,
    "dDurationMs": 7351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our 3-body problem video, they asked how we\ncan know that these galaxies will actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998120,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collide."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999120,
    "dDurationMs": 3769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then thanks us for making this video just\nto answer the question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002889,
    "dDurationMs": 1911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, you’re welcome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004800,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For those who missed the answer - we know that the collision will happen because we simulated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009740,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010740,
    "dDurationMs": 6409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To be less glib, we simulated it a LOT - covering all reasonable unknown properties of the system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017149,
    "dDurationMs": 8622,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No matter what we put in for those unknown properties, the Milky Way \u0026 Andromeda still do collide in about 4 Billion years. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025771,
    "dDurationMs": 7109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now Roli follows with another question: if we simulate a perfect universe containing beings"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032880,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that themselves are able to simulate a perfect\nuniverse; wouldn't that be ultimate proof"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037400,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we are in a simulation?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well I wouldn’t call that a proof - I’d\ncall it a demonstration of plausibility."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043560,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, if you follow the chain all the\nway up to the top layer - you have to have beings"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049990,
    "dDurationMs": 5939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who simulated a universe containing beings who could simulate a universe, but they themselves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055929,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were not simulated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057909,
    "dDurationMs": 3111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now couldn’t we be those top layer beings?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061020,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe we’re the bottom layer, and every\nlevel above is much more interesting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067490,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OR the simulations might run in a loop and\nthe top layer is simulated by the bottom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073630,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’d better get simulating or we’ll\nbreak the cycle."
    } ]
  } ]
}
